Why version 3, when the upstream project shipped the version 2 text
-------------------------------------------------------------------

This is an election made by this fork, and the reasoning is set out here so it
can be checked rather than taken on trust.

What "Convert to it!" actually contains, verified against the repository at
commit 2fc7143:

  - LICENSE is the stock GPLv2 text, with the "How to Apply These Terms"
    template placeholders left unfilled - no program name, no copyright line,
    no "version 2 or, at your option, any later version" election.
  - Its README has no License section.
  - Its package.json has no `license` field.
  - No source file carries a GPL header.
  - Its README does mention a version once, at line 33, in contributor
    guidance for proposing new formats: "make sure the license is compatible
    with GPL-2.0". That is a statement about which third-party libraries may
    be pulled in, not a statement of the program's own licence version.

GPLv2 section 9 provides:

    If the Program does not specify a version number of this License, you may
    choose any version ever published by the Free Software Foundation.

The election rests on reading that single README line as dependency-selection
guidance rather than as the Program specifying a version number "which applies
to it". That reading is defensible - it is not a licence grant, it appears in
issue-filing instructions, and none of the places a version election normally
lives is filled in - but it is a judgement call, and a reader should know it is
one. It is weaker than "upstream names no version at all", which is not true.

The safest way to remove the ambiguity entirely is to ask p2r3 to state a
version explicitly upstream, or to confirm they are content with this fork
being GPLv3. Until then this file records the position and the evidence for it.

The GPLv2 text this fork inherited is retained verbatim in
LICENSE.upstream-GPLv2. LICENSE is the GPLv3 text that governs frogConvert.

The election is not academic: GPLv3 section 13 permits combining a covered work
with AGPLv3 code, which is what allows the Ghostscript-based PDF compressor to
ship. Ghostscript is part of the Compress surface rather than an optional
extra, so AGPLv3 section 13's network-interaction requirement applies to the
combination - which is why the hosted app offers its own source. That offer
lives on the docs page, reachable from the footer's "view docs": a link to the
repository, and a link to the exact commit the running build came from, which
is the revision the source offer is actually about. The upstream Ghostscript
licence travels with the binary at /wasm/gs/LICENSE.

None of the above is legal advice.


